tel 1.0

base turbulence
import soundness

info   byte and text streams: one `read`, `writeTo` and `source` over every source and sink, with lines, stdio and async pumps
detail
    _Turbulence_ is the vocabulary for moving bytes and text between the things that produce
    them and the things that consume them. Three verbs cover every source and every sink:
    `read[T]` pulls a source in whole as the type named, `writeTo` drains a value into a
    destination, and `source[element]` opens a value as a pull endpoint — a zephyrine
    `Stream[Data]` or `Stream[Text]` over `Credit` — which later stages attach to with `via`
    and terminal operations drain. Which types take part is decided by typeclasses, so a new
    file type, socket or process joins the same three verbs as everything else:

    - `Streamable` says how a value opens as a stream of `Data` or `Text` chunks;
    - `Aggregable` says how a stream of `Data` or `Text` collapses into one value;
    - `Readable` composes the two into `read`, bridging bytes and text through the `Charset`
      or `Codepage` in scope when the source and the result disagree;
    - `Writable` says how a destination drains a whole stream, and `Sink` how it opens as a
      push endpoint (an `Intake`) for callers which write incrementally.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

    val text: Text = t"The quick brown fox\njumps over the lazy dog"
    val bytes: Data = text.read[Data]              // the UTF-8 bytes
    bytes.read[Text]                               // the text again
    bytes.source[Data].delineate.records.to[List]  // List(t"The quick brown fox", t"jumps over the lazy dog")
    ```

    A `Stream` is a pull endpoint over a buffer, not a lazy list of chunks: a consumer calls
    `refill` with the `Credit` it is prepared to take, reads the window it is granted, and
    skips past it. Nothing is copied or retained, backpressure is the absence of a call, and
    every stream is an exclusive capability which the capture checker will not let be read
    twice or shared. `Buffering` (zephyrine's) is the one policy every buffer consults, and
    the `Truncation.Error` a cut stream reports is zephyrine's too, re-exported here.

    Lines are a policy, not a constant: `delineate` splits a text or byte stream into a record
    stream of its lines under the `LineSeparation` given in scope, chosen by name from the
    `lineSeparation` package. Standard streams are a capability: `Out`, `Err` and `In` write
    and read through the `Stdio` in scope, chosen from the `stdios` package, and
    `Stdio.divert` and `Stdio.capture` redirect a thread's `System.out` and `System.err`.
    Where elements are produced over time by other threads, a `Relay` fans any number of
    producers into one reader; `Confluence` merges streams, `Divergence` replicates one, and
    `convey` pumps a stream into an intake on its own task.

    The library has four source sets:

    - `turbulence.core` — the typeclasses, the `read`/`writeTo`/`source`/`load` verbs, line
      separation, `Relay`, `StreamOutputStream` and the JDK adapters (`inputStream`, the
      `InputStream`/`OutputStream`/channel/`Reader` givens).
    - `turbulence.stdio` — `Stdio`, `Out`, `Err`, `In` and the `stdios` package's
      `muteStdio`, `javaLangSystemStdio` and `fileDescriptorStdio`.
    - `turbulence.async` — `Confluence`, `Divergence` and `convey`, which need a parasite
      `Monitor` and `Probate`, so they run inside `supervise`.
    - `turbulence.wasi` — `stdios.wasiStdio`, a `Stdio` over the WASI CLI stream resources,
      and `wasiApis.wasiCliApi`, the WIT interface it is typechecked against.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `lineSeparation`, `stdios`
    and `wasiApis` choice packages merge with the umbrella's own. Their givens are imported
    by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens; the companion givens
    (`Streamable.bytes`, `Readable.dataText`, `Writable.outputStreamData`, …) resolve
    through implicit scope with no import.

refer turbulence.Streamable
refer turbulence.Readable
refer turbulence.Writable
refer turbulence.Aggregable
refer turbulence.Sink
refer turbulence.LineSeparation
refer turbulence.Stdio
refer turbulence.Relay
refer zephyrine.Stream
refer zephyrine.Buffering
refer zephyrine.Truncation

entry .Streamable   a value which opens as a pull endpoint: a `Stream` of `Data` or `Text` over `Credit`
  detail
      `value is Streamable by medium over Credit` is the evidence that `value` can be opened
      as a `(Stream[medium] over Credit)^`, where `medium` is `Data` or `Text`. It is the
      source half of `read`, the source half of `writeTo`, and the whole of `source`. The
      trait has one abstract method, `#stream`; an instance which needs a buffer policy or an
      error context takes it as a contextual parameter of the given and captures it, which is
      why most givens here are written `(tactic: Tactic[Truncation.Error], buffering:
      Buffering) => (… is Streamable …)^{tactic}`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Row(name: Text, count: Int)

      object Row:
        given streamable: Row is Streamable by Text over Credit =
          row => Stream(t"${row.name},${row.count}")

      Row(t"apples", 3).source[Text]
      Row(t"apples", 3).read[Text]   // t"apples,3"
      ```

      Values which are streamable without any instance of their own: `Data` and `Text`
      (`.bytes`, `.text`, and `.textData` to stream text as bytes through the `Codepage` in
      scope), a `Chain[Data]` or `Chain[Text]` of chunks, an `HttpStreams.Body`, a
      zephyrine `Spring[Data]`, and the JDK's `InputStream`, `ReadableByteChannel` and
      `Reader`. A stream opened from a JDK source raises `Truncation.Error` through the
      `Tactic` the given captured if the source fails mid-read, and closes the source when it
      ends.

      Every type which streams by `Data` also reads as `Text` and vice versa: `Readable`
      bridges through the `Charset` or `Codepage` in scope, so one `Streamable` instance is
      enough for a source. `#contramap` derives an instance for a type which can be turned
      into one that already has an instance.
  refer turbulence.Readable
  refer turbulence.source
  refer zephyrine.Stream
  entry #Transport   the demand type the stream is pulled with: `Credit` for every instance here
  entry #stream   opens the value as a fresh pull endpoint
      Each call mints a new `(Stream[Operand] over Transport)^`; the stream is an exclusive
      capability, drained by one consumer. `value.source[element]` is the fluent form.
  entry #contramap   an instance for `self2`, by turning it into a `Self` first
      `streamable.contramap(lambda)` is `self2 is Streamable by Operand over Transport`,
      streaming `lambda(value)`. The result captures both the original instance and the
      lambda.
  entry .bytes   a `Data` streams as itself, in one window
      `Data is Streamable by Data over Credit`: `Stream(data)`, a region-stable stream whose
      single window is the array itself, shared by reference and never copied.
  entry .sizedBytes   a `Data & Sized[length]` streams as its bytes
      A sized block is a refinement of `Data` which the exact-`Self` `.bytes` instance does
      not match, so it has an instance of its own with the same behaviour.
  entry .transported   bytes tagged with a format, `Data over transport`, stream as plain bytes
      What `value.in[Data over Protobuf]` writes reads back through the ordinary byte
      pipeline: the tag is a phantom and the stream is `Stream(data)`.
  entry .text   a `Text` (or any subtype) streams as itself, in one window
      `textual is Streamable by Text over Credit` for every `textual <: Text`, so a refined
      or branded text needs no instance of its own.
  entry .textData   a `Text` streams as bytes, encoded through the `Codepage` in scope
      Needs a `Codepage` and a `Buffering`. The text is encoded incrementally through the
      encoder duct, so `text.source[Data]` and `text.read[Data]` never hold the whole
      encoding at once. With a `Codepage` in scope, `load` prefers this byte route for a
      `Text` source, as `read` does.
  entry .chainData   a lazy `Chain[Data]` of chunks is a byte source
      Each chunk becomes one window. The chain's production is beyond the stream's demand
      control — a `Chain` is produced by whoever built it — so credit bounds only how much of
      each chunk is exposed at a time.
  entry .chainText   a lazy `Chain[Text]` of chunks is a text source
  entry .httpBody   an `HttpStreams.Body` (the HTTP-body interchange protocol) is a byte source
      Needs a `Buffering`. Each refill asks the body for one block-sized chunk, so a request
      or response body is `read` directly, without first being materialised.
  entry .spring   a zephyrine `Spring[Data]` is a byte source, minting a fresh endpoint per pull
      Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, which the instance captures: a spring's `apply()`
      raises it when the source is cut, so a request body or a multipart part can be `read`
      or handed to a parser as it is.
  entry .inputStream   any `java.io.InputStream` is a byte source
      Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`. Reads go through a channel over
      the stream, directly into the stream's own storage, at most one granted block
      (`buffering.capacity(Substrate.Bytes)`) at a time. End of input closes the
      `InputStream`; an `IOException` mid-read closes it and aborts with `Truncation.Error`
      carrying the bytes delivered so far.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val input = java.io.ByteArrayInputStream(Array.unsafeJvm(t"hello".read[Data]))
      input.read[Text]   // t"hello"
      ```
  entry .channel   any `java.nio.channels.ReadableByteChannel` is a byte source
      As `.inputStream`, reading each block with `channel.read(buffer)`; needs a
      `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`.
  entry .reader   any `java.io.Reader` is a text source
      Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`. Characters are read in blocks of
      `buffering.capacity(Substrate.Chars)` into a char window; the reader is closed at end of
      input, and an `IOException` aborts with `Truncation.Error` carrying the characters
      delivered so far.

entry .Readable   a source which can be read whole as a result type: the composition behind `read[T]`
  detail
      `source is Readable to result` is what `value.read[result]` needs. It is almost never
      written by hand: `Readable`'s companion composes it from a `Streamable` instance for
      the source and an `Aggregable` instance for the result, bridging bytes and text through
      the `Charset` or `Codepage` in scope when the two disagree. The four pipelines are
      ordinary prioritised givens: `dataToData` in `Readable` itself is highest, then
      `textToText` in `Readable1`, `dataToText` in `Readable2` and `textToData` in `Readable3`.
      By source and result:

      ```tabular
      column  Source
      column  To a byte-aggregated result
      column  To a text-aggregated result
      row  bytes  `dataToData`  `dataToText`, decoded through a `Charset`; needs a `Buffering`
      row  text  `textToData`, encoded through a `Codepage`; needs a `Buffering`  `textToText`
      ```

      So a type with only a `Streamable by Data` instance reads as `Text`, and one with only
      `Streamable by Text` reads as `Data`, given an encoding; a type with both prefers the
      direct route. Because they are givens rather than a macro, a missing instance is an
      ordinary failed implicit search: the message is _turbulence: the source cannot be read
      as the target type; this needs a `Source` instance for the source and an `Aggregable`
      instance for the target, plus a `Charset` or `Codepage` if their operands (Data/Text)
      differ_.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val text = t"The quick brown fox"
      text.read[Text]          // t"The quick brown fox"
      text.read[Data]          // the UTF-8 bytes
      text.read[Chain[Text]]   // the text as a lazy chain of chunks
      text.read[Data].read[Text]
      ```

      A whole `Data` in hand has direct instances (`.dataData`, `.dataText`) which decode in
      one step rather than through a one-window stream, which is what makes a file's
      `path.read[Text]` a genuinely direct read. The bridged pipelines transcode
      incrementally, in bounded buffers, on the reading thread.
  refer turbulence.read
  refer turbulence.Streamable
  refer turbulence.Aggregable
  entry #read   reads the value whole as the result type
  entry .dataData   a `Data` reads as itself, with no copy
  entry .dataText   a `Data` reads as `Text`, decoded whole through the `Charset` in scope
  entry .sizedDataData   a `Data & Sized[length]` reads as `Data`
      A sized block is a refinement the exact-`Self` `.dataData` does not match, so it has
      its own instance.
  entry .sizedDataText   a `Data & Sized[length]` reads as `Text` through the `Charset` in scope
  entry .dataToData   a byte-streamable source reads as any byte-aggregable result
      The highest-priority composed pipeline: `source is Streamable by Data over Credit` with
      `result is Aggregable by Data`, no encoding needed. The result captures both instances.

entry .Readable1   the second-priority `read` pipeline: a text source to a text result
  hidden
  detail
      A priority layer of `Readable`'s companion, not for direct use: it holds the pipeline
      which `Readable.dataToData` outranks and which outranks the two bridged ones.
  entry #textToText   a text-streamable source reads as any text-aggregable result
      `source is Streamable by Text over Credit` with `result is Aggregable by Text`; no
      encoding is needed.

entry .Readable2   the third-priority `read` pipeline: a byte source decoded to a text result
  hidden
  detail
      A priority layer of `Readable`'s companion, not for direct use.
  entry #dataToText   a byte-streamable source reads as a text-aggregable result through a `Charset`
      Needs a `Charset` and a `Buffering`: the bytes are decoded incrementally through the
      charset duct, so `bytes.read[Text]` on a stream never holds the whole decoding at once.

entry .Readable3   the lowest-priority `read` pipeline: a text source encoded to a byte result
  hidden
  detail
      A priority layer of `Readable`'s companion, not for direct use.
  entry #textToData   a text-streamable source reads as a byte-aggregable result through a `Codepage`
      Needs a `Codepage` and a `Buffering`: the text is encoded incrementally through the
      codepage duct.

entry .Aggregable   a result type which can be built from a stream of `Data` or `Text` chunks
  detail
      `result is Aggregable by medium` is the result half of `read[result]`: how a whole
      stream of `Data` or `Text` collapses into one value. `Data` and `Text` are aggregable
      by their own medium (`.bytesData`, `.textText`), `Text` also by `Data` through the
      `Charset` in scope (`.bytesText`), and a `Chain[element]` of anything aggregable is
      aggregable (`.stream`), which is how `read[Chain[Text]]` works.

      The trait has two methods. `#aggregate` takes the legacy shape, a lazy `Chain` of
      chunks; `#accept` takes a pull endpoint and, by default, materialises one chunk per
      refill and delegates to `aggregate`. The `Data` and `Text` instances override `accept`
      to copy each window straight into a builder, with no intermediate chunks, and a format
      whose `accept` streams should do the same. `#map` derives an instance for a type
      reachable from an aggregated one, forwarding `accept` as well as `aggregate` so that a
      streaming `accept` is not lost under the mapping.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Shout(text: Text)

      object Shout:
        given aggregable: Shout is Aggregable by Text =
          summon[Text is Aggregable by Text].map: text =>
            Shout(text.upper)

      t"the quick brown fox".read[Shout]   // Shout(t"THE QUICK BROWN FOX")
      ```
  refer turbulence.Readable
  refer turbulence.read
  entry #aggregate   builds the result from a lazy `Chain` of chunks
  entry #accept   builds the result by draining a pull endpoint
      The default refills with unbounded credit, materialises each window as a chunk and
      hands the lazy chain of them to `#aggregate`. Override it to read the windows directly.
  entry #map   an instance for `self2`, by transforming the aggregated `Self`
      The mapped instance forwards both `accept` and `aggregate`, and captures the original
      instance and the lambda.
  entry .bytesData   a stream of `Data` aggregates to one `Data`
      Copies each window into a growing byte builder, one copy per region, and builds the
      result once. The `aggregate` form sums the chunk lengths and places each chunk.
  entry .bytesText   a stream of `Data` aggregates to `Text`, decoded through the `Charset` in scope
      `.bytesData` mapped through `Charset#decoded`: the bytes are gathered whole and decoded
      in one step.
  entry .textText   a stream of `Text` aggregates to one `Text`
  entry .stream   a `Chain[element2]` is aggregable by `element` wherever `element2` is
      Each chunk is aggregated on its own, so `read[Chain[Text]]` yields one element per
      chunk of the source rather than one joined value.

entry .Writable   a destination which drains a whole stream of `Data` or `Text` chunks
  detail
      `target is Writable by medium` is what `value.writeTo(target)` needs on the target's
      side: a single method, `#write`, which takes the target and a pull endpoint and drains
      it to completion. The JDK's `OutputStream` and `WritableByteChannel` are writable by
      `Data` (and `OutputStream` by `Text`, through the `Codepage` in scope), and an adapter
      pair makes any `Writable by Text` also `Writable by Data` and vice versa, so a
      destination needs one instance for whichever medium is natural to it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      class Store():
        val buffer: StringBuilder = StringBuilder()

      object Store:
        given writable: Store is Writable by Text = (store, stream) =>
          stream.asInstanceOf[AnyRef].asInstanceOf[(Stream[Text] over Credit)^].drain:
            region => range => store.buffer.append(region.materialize(range).s)

      val store = Store()
      t"hello".writeTo(store)
      t"hello".read[Data].writeTo(store)   // bytes, through the decoding adapter
      ```

      A write failure is reported, not thrown: the JDK givens take an `Emit[Truncation.Error]`
      and `raise` the error with the count of bytes written before the cut, then stop
      writing. The target is closed whether or not the write failed. A writer never aborts,
      so a `Writable` given is summoned under an `Emit`, never a `Tactic`.

      `Sink` is the push-side counterpart, for callers which write incrementally through an
      `Intake` instead of handing over a whole stream. The `write` method's stream parameter
      is not declared `consume`, so an instance which hands it to a consuming operation
      crosses it as a neutral reference, as the sample does.
  refer turbulence.writeTo
  refer turbulence.Sink
  refer zephyrine.Truncation
  entry #write   drains the stream into the target, to completion
  entry #contramap   an instance for `self2`, by turning it into a `Self` first
  entry .outputStreamData   any `java.io.OutputStream` is writable by `Data`
      Needs an `Emit[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`. Each window is written straight
      from the stream's own storage; the stream is flushed and closed at the end, and an
      `IOException` at any point raises `Truncation.Error` with the bytes written so far.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val output = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
      t"hello".read[Data].writeTo(output)
      output.toByteArray.nn.length   // 5
      ```
  entry .outputStreamText   a `java.io.OutputStream` is writable by `Text`, encoded through the `Codepage` in scope
      As `.outputStreamData`, with the text encoded incrementally through the codepage duct;
      needs a `Codepage` as well.
  entry .decodingAdapter   a target writable by `Text` is writable by `Data`, through the `Charset` in scope
      The bytes are decoded incrementally through the charset duct before reaching the text
      writer; needs a `Charset` and a `Buffering`.
  entry .encodingAdapter   a target writable by `Data` is writable by `Text`, through the `Codepage` in scope
      The text is encoded incrementally through the codepage duct before reaching the byte
      writer; needs a `Codepage` and a `Buffering`.
  entry .chunked   a target writable by a medium is writable by a record stream of that medium's chunks
      `writable is Writable by (Array[medium]^{})`: each record of the stream is one chunk,
      written in order. This is the shape of an event log, where every formatted event is one
      `Text` or `Data` chunk, and is what a `Logger`'s destination needs. Needs the medium's
      `Addressable` and a `Buffering`.
  entry .channel   a `java.nio.channels.WritableByteChannel` is writable by `Data`
      Needs an `Emit[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`. Each window is wrapped as a
      `ByteBuffer` and written until drained; a `-1` from the channel or an exception raises
      `Truncation.Error` with the bytes written so far. The channel is not closed.

entry .Sink   a destination which opens as a push endpoint: an `Intake` of `Data` or `Text` over `Credit`
  detail
      `target is Sink by medium over Credit` is the push-side counterpart of `Writable`:
      rather than draining a whole stream, `#intake` opens the target as a zephyrine
      `(Intake[medium] over Credit)^`, which a caller writes to incrementally with `put`, and
      which a stream is pumped into with `pump` or, on its own task, `convey`. `finish`
      closes the underlying resource, as `Writable` does at the end of a stream.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val output = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
      val sink = summon[(java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream is Sink by Data over Credit)^]
      t"hello".read[Data].source[Data].pump(sink.intake(output))
      output.toByteArray.nn.length   // 5
      ```

      A write failure is reported through the `Emit[Truncation.Error]` the given captured —
      a sink only reports a cut, never aborts — and the intake's demand drops to zero so
      that a pump stops. `.buffered` adapts a whole-chain writing function to the intake
      protocol for a target which has only that.
  refer turbulence.Writable
  refer turbulence.convey
  refer zephyrine.Intake
  entry #Transport   the demand type of the intake: `Credit` for every instance here
  entry #intake   opens the target as a fresh push endpoint
  entry #contramap   an instance for `self2`, by turning it into a `Self` first
  entry .outputStream   any `java.io.OutputStream` opens as a byte intake
      Needs an `Emit[Truncation.Error]` and a `Buffering`. Writes are staged in a block of
      `buffering.capacity(Substrate.Bytes)` bytes and written through (and flushed) when it
      fills, on `flush`, and on `finish`, which then closes the stream. An `IOException`
      marks the intake broken — its demand becomes zero — and raises `Truncation.Error` with
      the bytes written so far.
  entry .channel   a `java.nio.channels.WritableByteChannel` opens as a byte intake
      As `.outputStream`, writing each staged block through a `ByteBuffer`; `finish` closes
      the channel.
  entry .buffered   an intake which accumulates chunks and hands them all to a whole-chain writer on `finish`
      `Sink.buffered(target, write)` takes a `(target, Chain[medium]) => Unit` and returns an
      `(Intake[medium] over Credit)^`: writes are staged by the `Buffering` block size, each
      full block becomes one chunk, and `finish` calls `write` once with every chunk in
      order. Its demand is honestly unbounded, since it keeps everything until `finish`. It
      needs the medium's `Addressable` and a `Buffering`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      var sizes: List[Int] = Nil
      val intake = Sink.buffered[Unit, Data]((), (_, chunks) => sizes = chunks.map(_.length).to[List])
      intake.put(Data.fill(8)(_.toByte))
      intake.finish()
      ```

entry .Loadable   a document type which parses from a stream of `Data` or `Text`, keeping its metadata
  detail
      `result is Loadable by medium` is what `value.load[result]` needs on the result's
      side: `#load` takes a pull endpoint and returns a `Document[result]`, the parsed root
      beside the metadata the parse discovered (an XML prologue's encoding, an HTML
      document's source positions). The `Self` must be a `Documentary`, which declares what
      its metadata is. Formats such as `Xml`, `Html`, `Tel` and `Math` provide instances,
      usually one by `Text` and one by `Data`, each capturing the `Tactic` for its own parse
      error.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      val document: Document[Xml] = t"<doc><a/></doc>".load[Xml]
      document()         // the parsed Xml root
      document.metadata
      ```

      Where a source and a loader disagree on medium, `load` bridges through the `Charset`
      or `Codepage` in scope; `.fromData` and `.fromText` are the two halves of that
      dispatch, for code which already holds a stream.
  refer turbulence.load
  refer turbulence.Document
  refer turbulence.Documentary
  entry #Self   the document type, a `Documentary`
  entry #Operand   the medium the loader reads: `Data` or `Text`
  entry #load   parses the stream into a `Document[Self]`
  entry .fromData   loads a document from a byte stream, decoding through a `Charset` if the loader wants text
      `Loadable.fromData[result](stream)` consumes the stream. It prefers a `result is
      Loadable by Data`; failing that it uses a `Loadable by Text` through the `Charset` and
      `Buffering` in scope; and with neither it fails to compile with _turbulence: the result
      type has no `Loadable` instance_.
  entry .fromText   loads a document from a text stream, encoding through a `Codepage` if the loader wants bytes
      The text twin of `.fromData`: a `Loadable by Text` first, else `Loadable by Data`
      through the `Codepage` and `Buffering` in scope.
  entry .whole   a whole-value source's bytes as a one-window stream
      `Loadable.whole(source)` reads the source with its `Readable to Data` instance and
      wraps the result as a `Stream[Data]`: the route `load` takes for a source which is not
      streamable but is readable whole, such as a filesystem path.

entry .Document   a parsed document: its root value beside the metadata the parse discovered
  detail
      `Document[content]` is what `load[content]` returns. `#root` (also `#apply`) is the
      parsed value, and `#metadata` is typed by the content's own `Documentary` declaration:
      `root.Metadata`, so an `Xml` document's metadata is whatever `Xml` says it is.
  refer turbulence.Loadable
  refer turbulence.load
  entry #root   the parsed document
  entry #metadata   what the parse discovered beside the content, typed as `root.Metadata`
  entry #apply   the parsed document, as `document()`

entry .Documentary   a type which can be the content of a `Document`, declaring its metadata type
  detail
      A marker trait for a format's root type: `type Metadata` is what a parse of it discovers
      beside the content, and `Document[content]` exposes it as `root.Metadata`. `Xml`,
      `Html`, `Tel`, `Email` and `Math` are documentaries; a new format extends it and gives a
      `Loadable` instance.
  refer turbulence.Document
  refer turbulence.Loadable
  entry #Self   the documentary type itself
  entry #Metadata   what a parse of this type discovers beside its content

entry .Eof   a file named for appending: `Eof(file)` is the file's end
  detail
      `Eof(path)` wraps a path so that opening it means opening the file for appending. The
      filesystem library makes `Eof[file]` openable wherever `file` is, prepending the `Append`
      flag, so `Eof(path).open(...)` appends where `path.open(...)` would overwrite. The
      wrapper itself is just the case class with its one field.
  entry #file   the wrapped file

entry .Line   one line of text, which renders with the newline the `LineSeparation` in scope uses
  detail
      `Line(content)` holds a line without its terminator; `line()` appends the newline
      sequence of the `LineSeparation` given in scope, so one value renders as `\n`, `\r\n`
      or `\r` according to the policy chosen.
  refer turbulence.LineSeparation
  entry #content   the line's text, without a terminator
  entry #apply   the line with the `LineSeparation`'s newline appended

entry .LineSeparation   a line-ending policy: which of CR, LF, CRLF and LFCR end a line, and how a line is written
  detail
      A `LineSeparation` says how `delineate` splits text into lines, and which sequence
      `Line#apply` and `#newlineData` write. It is a value of five fields: `#newline`, the
      `NewlineSeq` written out, and four `Action`s saying what a bare CR, a bare LF, a CRLF
      pair and an LFCR pair each do when read — end a line (`Nl`), stay in the text as a
      character (`Cr`, `Lf`), be dropped (`Skip`), or end a line and keep part of themselves
      (`NlCr`, `NlLf`, `CrNl`, `LfNl`) or end two (`NlNl`).

      The policy is never implicit: the `lineSeparation` package has the standard ones,
      imported by name, and the same text splits differently under each.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Adaptive: any of CR, LF, CRLF or LFCR ends a line
        code
            locally:
              import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation
              t"a\r\nb\rc\nd".delineate.to[List]   // List(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d")
      frame
        label Linefeeds: LF ends a line and a bare CR is dropped
        code
            locally:
              import lineSeparation.linefeedLineSeparation
              t"a\r\nb\rc\nd".delineate.to[List]   // List(t"a", t"bc", t"d")
      frame
        label Carriage returns: CR ends a line and a bare LF is dropped
        code
            locally:
              import lineSeparation.carriageReturnLineSeparation
              t"a\r\nb\rc\nd".delineate.to[List]   // List(t"a", t"b", t"cd")
      frame
        label CRLF only: a bare CR is dropped and a bare LF stays in the text
        code
            locally:
              import lineSeparation.carriageReturnLinefeedLineSeparation
              t"a\r\nb\rc\nd".delineate.to[List]   // List(t"a", t"bc\nd")
      ##
      ```

      The splitting itself is a zephyrine `Duct`, applied as a stage: `.lines` is the
      `Ductile` instance which lets a policy value be attached to a text stream with `via`,
      yielding a record stream with one `Text` per line, without its terminator. A final
      line with no terminator is still delivered; an empty input yields no lines; and a
      two-character sequence split across two windows is resolved across the boundary. A
      byte stream under an ASCII-transparent encoding (UTF-8, ASCII, Latin-1) is split in the
      raw bytes by `.byteLines`, each line decoded whole in one step, which `delineate`
      chooses by checking the `Charset`; any other encoding decodes first.

      A line with no separator at all grows the carried partial line without bound, as any
      line reader must; cap the input upstream if it is adversarial.
  refer turbulence.delineate
  refer turbulence.lineSeparation
  refer turbulence.Line
  entry .NewlineSeq   the sequence a policy writes as a line ending: `Cr`, `Lf`, `CrLf` or `LfCr`
    detail
        An enum of the four newline sequences; `#text` is the sequence as `Text`
        (`"\r"`, `"\n"`, `"\r\n"`, `"\n\r"`), and `LineSeparation#newlineData` gives the same as
        bytes.
    entry #text   the sequence as text
  entry .Action   what a policy does with a CR, LF, CRLF or LFCR it reads
      An enum of nine actions: `Nl` ends the line; `Cr` and `Lf` keep the character in the
      line's text; `Skip` drops it; `NlCr` and `NlLf` end the line and start the next with
      the character; `CrNl` and `LfNl` append the character and then end the line; and
      `NlNl` ends two lines. A `LineSeparation` names one for each of its `cr`, `lf`, `crlf`
      and `lfcr` fields.
  entry .lines   the `Ductile` instance which applies a policy to a text stream as a stage
      `stream.via(lineSeparation)` attaches the line-splitting duct, producing a
      `Stream[Array[Text]^{}]` — a record stream with one `Text` per line — and translating
      line credit to character credit one for one. `delineate` is the same with the policy
      taken from scope. It needs a `Buffering`.
  entry .asciiTransparent   whether a `0x0A` or `0x0D` byte in a charset can only be a line terminator
      True for UTF-8, US-ASCII and ISO-8859-1, in which no multi-byte sequence contains those
      bytes, so lines can be found before decoding. False for UTF-16 and the rest.
  entry .byteLines   the byte-level line duct for an ASCII-transparent encoding
      `LineSeparation.byteLines(policy, charset)` builds a `Duct[Data, Array[Text]^{}]` which
      finds terminators in the raw bytes and decodes each completed line in one fused call,
      with no separate decoding stage; a multi-byte character cut by a window boundary simply
      accumulates with the line. It is a method rather than a given, applied by `delineate`
      once it has checked the encoding with `.asciiTransparent`, and must not be used with an
      encoding that fails that check. Needs a `Buffering`.
  entry #newline   the sequence written as a line ending
  entry #cr   what a bare carriage return does when read
  entry #lf   what a bare linefeed does when read
  entry #crlf   what a carriage return followed by a linefeed does when read
  entry #lfcr   what a linefeed followed by a carriage return does when read
  entry #newlineData   the newline sequence as bytes
      `Data(13)`, `Data(10)`, `Data(13, 10)` or `Data(10, 13)` for `Cr`, `Lf`, `CrLf` and
      `LfCr`.

entry .lineSeparation   the standard line-ending policies, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which characters end a line is decided by the `LineSeparation` given in scope, and the
      choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is
      imported by name. Each is a `LineSeparation` value whose action table the test suite
      runs through every fragmentation of its input.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

      t"one two\nthree four\r\nfive".delineate.to[List]   // List(t"one two", t"three four", t"five")
      ```

      `adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation` suits input of unknown provenance; the strict
      policies are for formats where a lone character of the other kind is data, and
      `javaBaseLineSeparation` follows the running platform.
  refer turbulence.LineSeparation
  refer turbulence.delineate
  entry .adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation   any of CR, LF, CRLF or LFCR ends a line; writes LF
      Every sequence is one line break, so `a\n\nb` is three lines (`a`, an empty line, `b`)
      and `a\r\nb` two.
  entry .linefeedLineSeparation   LF ends a line; a bare CR is dropped; CRLF and LFCR are one break; writes LF
      `a\rb` is the one line `ab`.
  entry .strictLinefeedsLineSeparation   a bare CR ends a line and an LF stays in the text; writes LF
      The action table as it stands is `cr = Nl, lf = Lf, crlf = NlLf, lfcr = LfNl` — the
      same as `strictCarriageReturnLineSeparation` — so `a\nb` is the one line `a\nb` and
      `a\rb` is two; only the written newline (LF) differs.
  entry .carriageReturnLineSeparation   CR ends a line; a bare LF is dropped; CRLF and LFCR are one break; writes CR
      `a\nb` is the one line `ab`.
  entry .strictCarriageReturnLineSeparation   only CR ends a line; an LF stays in the text; writes CR
      `a\r\nb` is the two lines `a` and `\nb`.
  entry .carriageReturnLinefeedLineSeparation   only CRLF ends a line; a bare CR is dropped and a bare LF kept; writes CRLF
      `a\nb` is the one line `a\nb`, `a\rb` is `ab`, and `a\n\rb` is `a\n` and `b`.
  entry .javaBaseLineSeparation   the policy matching the JVM's `System.lineSeparator`
      `carriageReturnLinefeedLineSeparation` on Windows, `linefeedLineSeparation` where the
      separator is LF, `carriageReturnLineSeparation` for CR, and otherwise the adaptive
      policy.

entry .source   opens a value as a pull endpoint, naming the element type: `value.source[Data]`
  detail
      An extension on any value with a `Streamable by element over Credit` instance:
      `value.source[Text]` or `value.source[Data]` is `streamable.stream(value)`, a fresh
      `(Stream[element] over Credit)^`. It is the fluent form of `Streamable#stream`, named
      `source` because zephyrine's `.stream` constructor owns that name under the umbrella.
      The element type is given explicitly where a value is streamable both ways, as `Text`
      is with a `Codepage` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      t"The quick brown fox".source[Text].memoize
      t"The quick brown fox".source[Data].memoize
      ```
  refer turbulence.Streamable

entry .read   reads a value whole as the type named: `value.read[Text]`, `value.read[Data]`
  detail
      An inline extension on any value with a `Readable to result` instance. A source with a
      `Streamable` instance by either medium reads as `Data`, as `Text`, or as a
      `Chain[Data]` or `Chain[Text]`, or as any type with an `Aggregable` instance, with the
      `Charset` or `Codepage` in scope bridging bytes and text. A file, a URL, a process's
      output and a classpath resource all read with this one verb.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"The quick brown fox".read[Data].read[Text]   // t"The quick brown fox"
      ```

      A JDK source raises `Truncation.Error` through the `Tactic` its `Streamable` given
      captured if it is cut mid-read; a `Data` or `Text` in hand reads without a stream at all.
  refer turbulence.Readable

entry .writeTo   drains a value into a destination: `value.writeTo(target)`
  detail
      An extension on any value with a `Streamable by element over Credit` instance, for any
      target with a `Writable by element` instance: `value.writeTo(target)` streams the value
      and hands the stream to the target's writer, which drains it to completion and closes
      the target. The element type is inferred from the two instances, and the `Writable`
      adapters let text go to a byte sink and bytes to a text sink through the encoding in
      scope. A JDK target reports a cut with `Truncation.Error` through its `Emit`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val output = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
      t"The quick brown fox".read[Data].writeTo(output)
      ```
  refer turbulence.Writable
  refer turbulence.Streamable

entry .load   parses a value as a document type, keeping the metadata: `value.load[Xml]`
  detail
      An inline extension on any value: `value.load[result]` returns a `Document[result]` for
      any `result <: Documentary` with a `Loadable` instance. It resolves its instances at the
      call site, in order: a byte-streamable source with a byte loader, else a text loader
      through the `Charset` in scope; a text-streamable source with a text loader, else a byte
      loader through the `Codepage` in scope; and finally a source which is not streamable but
      is `Readable to Data` (a filesystem path), read whole. A `Text` with a `Codepage` in
      scope therefore takes the byte route, as `read` does. Each loader captures the `Tactic`
      for its own parse error, which must be in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      t"<doc><a/></doc>".load[Xml]()
      ```
  refer turbulence.Loadable
  refer turbulence.Document

entry .delineate   splits text or bytes into lines under the `LineSeparation` in scope
  detail
      Four overloads, one implementation. On a `(Stream[Text] over Credit)^` it attaches the
      `LineSeparation.lines` duct and yields a `(Stream[Array[Text]^{}] over Credit)^` — a
      record stream with one `Text` per line, without its terminator — to drain with
      `records`, `memoize`, or compose onward. On a `(Stream[Data] over Credit)^` it needs a
      `Charset` too: under an ASCII-transparent encoding (UTF-8, ASCII, Latin-1) the
      separators are found in the raw bytes and each line is decoded whole, in one fused call;
      under any other encoding the stream is decoded first and the characters split. On a
      whole `Text` or `Data` it drives the same duct over the value as a single window and
      returns an `Array[Text]^{}` directly. Every form needs a `LineSeparation` (imported by
      name from `lineSeparation`) and a `Buffering`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A byte stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import charsets.utf8Charset
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

            val bytes: Data = t"first\nsecond\r\nthird".read[Data]
            bytes.source[Data]
      frame
        label Split into lines, as a record stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import charsets.utf8Charset
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

            val bytes: Data = t"first\nsecond\r\nthird".read[Data]
            bytes.source[Data].delineate
      frame
        label Drained as records
        code
            import soundness.*
            import charsets.utf8Charset
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

            val bytes: Data = t"first\nsecond\r\nthird".read[Data]
            bytes.source[Data].delineate.records.to[List]   // List(t"first", t"second", t"third")
      frame
        label Or written out, one line per record
        code
            import soundness.*
            import charsets.utf8Charset
            import codepages.utf8Codepage
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import lineSeparation.adaptiveLinefeedLineSeparation

            val bytes: Data = t"first\nsecond\r\nthird".read[Data]
            val output = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
            bytes.source[Data].delineate.records.map(_ + t"\n").to[Chain].writeTo(output)
      ##
      ```

      A line spanning many windows is reassembled, a final line with no terminator is
      delivered, and an empty input yields no lines. The name is `delineate` rather than
      `lines` because gossamer's value-level `lines` would shadow a same-named extension under
      the umbrella.
  refer turbulence.LineSeparation
  refer turbulence.lineSeparation

entry .inputStream   a byte stream as a `java.io.InputStream`, for handing to JDK APIs
    Two forms. On a `(Stream[Data] over Credit)^` (needing a `Buffering`), the result pulls
    through the window one block of credit at a time and never materialises a chunk;
    `close` closes the endpoint, and so does reaching its end. On a `Chain[Data]`, the
    result walks the chain's chunks; its `close` does nothing. Both are the bridge to a Java
    library which insists on an `InputStream`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val input: java.io.InputStream = t"hello".source[Data].inputStream
    input.read()   // 104
    ```

entry .httpBody   a byte stream as an `HttpStreams.Body`, the HTTP-body interchange protocol
    An extension on a `(Stream[Data] over Credit)^` (consumed): each `next(limit)` of the
    body refills the stream with `limit` credit and materialises the region delivered, and
    returns `null` once the stream ends. The inverse of the `stream` extension on a body.
    Not exported to the umbrella: reach it as `turbulence.httpBody`.

entry .stream   an `HttpStreams.Body` as a pull endpoint, one chunk per block-sized request
    An extension on `HttpStreams.Body`, needing a `Buffering`: each refill asks the body for
    at most `buffering.capacity(Substrate.Bytes)` bytes (or the credit granted, if smaller)
    and exposes the chunk as the window; a `null` from the body ends the stream. It is what
    `Streamable.httpBody` uses, and it is not exported to the umbrella, where zephyrine's
    `stream` constructor owns the name: reach it as `turbulence.stream`.

entry .more   the rest of a chain inside a `flow` block
  detail
      `more` is the contextual `Chain` labelled `"more"` which `flow` binds beside `next`: the
      tail still to be consumed. It is an inline accessor which resolves the labelled value,
      so inside `stream.flow(end)(... more ...)` it reads as a plain name.
  refer turbulence.flow

entry .flow   a one-step case split over a chain: a termination value, or a block over `next` and `more`
  detail
      `chain.flow(termination)(proceed)` evaluates `proceed` with `next` (the head) and `more`
      (the tail) bound as contextual values if the chain is non-empty, and `termination`
      otherwise. It is the pattern `case next #:: more => … case _ => …` as a combinator,
      which `condense`, `drop` and `take` are written with. Under capture checking the
      labelled bindings may need `next.asInstanceOf[element]` in some positions.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def head(chain: Chain[Int]): Optional[Int] = chain.flow(Unset)(next)
      ```
  refer turbulence.more

entry .condense   collapses each run of consecutive equal elements of a chain to one, lazily
    `Chain(1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 1).condense` is `Chain(1, 2, 1)`: only adjacent duplicates are
    merged, which is distinct from `deduplicate`, the whole-collection distinct-by-key. The
    chain stays lazy.

entry .strict   forces a lazy chain to its end and returns it
    Evaluates every element of the chain (by taking its size) and returns the same chain,
    now fully memoized, so that whatever side effects its production had have happened.

entry .drop   drops a number of bytes from the front of a `Chain[Data]`
    `chain.drop(bytes)` takes a `Bytes` count rather than a chunk count: whole chunks within
    the count are skipped and the chunk which straddles the boundary is cut, so the result is
    the same bytes re-chunked at the boundary. Lazy.

entry .take   keeps a number of bytes from the front of a `Chain[Data]`
    `chain.take(bytes)` takes a `Bytes` count: chunks within the count are kept whole and
    the one which straddles the boundary is cut, so the result holds exactly `bytes` bytes
    (or fewer if the chain is shorter). Lazy.

entry .shred   re-chunks a `Chain[Data]` into buffers whose sizes are drawn afresh for each
    `chain.shred(chunkSize)` evaluates the by-name `chunkSize` once per output chunk (at
    least 1), so a caller hands in a constant to re-block a stream uniformly, or samples a
    distribution to produce every fragmentation a parser must survive:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import randomization.seededRandomization

    given Seed = Seed(1L)
    val data: Chain[Data] = Chain(Data.fill(1000)(_.toByte))

    stochastic:
      given Distribution = Gamma.approximate(20.0, 10.0)
      data.shred(arbitrary[Double]().toInt).map(_.length).total   // 1000
    ```

    The bytes are copied into fresh buffers, so the output never aliases the input's chunks.

entry .Relay   a bus from any number of producer threads to one reader of records
  detail
      A `Relay[record]` is the push-side bridge for record-granularity traffic — events,
      notifications, messages: producers `#put` records as they arise, one of them
      eventually calls `#stop`, and a single reader drains them through `#stream`, `#chain`
      or `#batches`. Producers never block, by contract rather than oversight: a relay is the
      bus which fans many writers into one drain (HTTP/2's outbound frame multiplexer relies
      on it, as does the logging spool), where a blocking `put` would risk deadlock between
      streams, so its queue is unbounded and its discipline is its single consumer. A bounded
      hand-off for byte or character traffic between two threads is zephyrine's `Conduit`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val relay = Relay[Text]()
      relay.put(t"one")
      relay.put(t"two")
      relay.stop()
      relay.stream.records.to[List]   // List(t"one", t"two")
      ```

      The reader blocks for the first record and then takes whatever else has already
      arrived into the same window, so records batch across the thread boundary instead of
      costing one hand-off each. Records put after `stop` are never delivered. Order is
      preserved per producer.
  refer zephyrine.Conduit
  refer turbulence.Confluence
  entry #put   adds a record; never blocks
  entry #stop   ends the relay: the reader sees the end once every record put before it is drained
  entry #stream   the pull endpoint over the records, as a record stream: single-owner, created once
      `(Stream[Array[record]^{}] over Credit)^`, needing the record array's `Addressable` (the
      generic boxed instance serves any reference type) and a `Buffering`. A refill blocks
      for a first record and then drains every record already queued into the same window,
      up to its capacity; a stop found mid-drain still delivers the batch and the next
      refill ends the stream. Drain it with `records`.
  entry #chain   the records as a lazy `Chain`, one cons cell per record
      Needs no `Addressable`, so it works for any record type including an abstract one, at
      the cost of a cell per record: the form for low-rate delivery such as logging or a
      message bus. Lazy and single-owner, as `#stream` is.
  entry #batches   the records coalesced into batches by a quiet period
      `relay.batches(quiet)` takes any duration abstractable to milliseconds (a `Long` with
      `abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable`, or an aviation duration) and returns a lazy
      `Chain[List[record]]`. The reader blocks for a first record; every further arrival
      restarts a timer of `quiet`; when it expires with nothing new, the records gathered so
      far are one batch, oldest first, and the reader blocks again. This is the "changed, and
      then nothing for a while" shape a rebuild or a rehash wants. A stop found mid-burst
      still delivers the partial batch, and a zero `quiet` degrades to draining whatever has
      already arrived.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import abstractables.millisecondsAbstractable

      val relay = Relay[Text]()
      relay.put(t"a")
      relay.put(t"b")
      relay.stop()
      relay.batches(50L).to[List]   // List(List(t"a", t"b"))
      ```

entry .StreamOutputStream   a `java.io.OutputStream` whose writes become a lazy `Chain[Data]`
  detail
      For a Java API which can only write to an `OutputStream`: hand it a
      `StreamOutputStream`, and read what it wrote from `#stream`, a `Chain[Data]` fed through
      a `Relay`. Single-byte writes are buffered and released as a chunk on `flush`; an array
      write is copied — the JDK lets a caller reuse its array once `write` returns, and the
      chunk is read later — and relayed as one chunk; `close` flushes and ends the chain. A
      reader of `stream` on another thread blocks until the next chunk or the close.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val out = StreamOutputStream()
      out.write(scala.Array[Byte](1, 2, 3))
      out.close()
      out.stream.read[Data].to[List]   // List(1, 2, 3)
      ```
  refer turbulence.Relay
  entry #stream   the bytes written so far and still to come, as a lazy `Chain[Data]`
  entry #write   writes one byte (buffered until `flush`), or an array, copied and relayed as a chunk

entry .Truncation   `zephyrine.Truncation`, re-exported: the error a stream raises when it is cut short
  detail
      `Truncation.Error(total)` is error 264, _the stream was cut prematurely after `total`_,
      where `total` is the `Bytes` delivered before the cut. A `Spring`'s pull and the
      `Streamable` givens for JDK sources abort with it through a `Tactic`; the `Writable` and
      `Sink` givens for JDK targets `raise` it through an `Emit`, since a writer only reports a
      cut and never aborts. The type lives in zephyrine, beside `Stream` and `Spring`; this
      export keeps `import turbulence.*` sufficient for modules which name it.
  refer zephyrine.Truncation

entry .Stdio   the standard streams as a value: `out`, `err`, `in` and the terminal's capabilities
  detail
      A `Stdio` holds the three standard streams as JDK `PrintStream`s and an `InputStream`,
      together with the `Termcap` describing what the terminal at the other end can render.
      It is a contextual value rather than a global: `Out`, `Err` and `In` write and read
      through the `Stdio` in scope, so a daemon serving several clients, a test capturing
      output, or a process launched with a pipe each get the streams that belong to them.
      One is chosen by name from the `stdios` package (`javaLangSystemStdio` for the JVM's
      own streams, `fileDescriptorStdio` for the process's descriptors, `muteStdio` for
      none, `wasiStdio` on WASI), built with `.apply`, or provided by a `Stdio.Provider`
      such as a CLI invocation, which `.provided` turns into a `Stdio` wherever the
      invocation is ambient.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap

      Out.println(t"started")
      Err.println(t"a warning")
      summon[Stdio].platform   // true
      ```

      A `Stdio` is a plain value, not a capability trait: it is mixed into exclusive types
      such as an `Invocation` as an interface, and one built from the JVM's thread-safe
      streams captures nothing. One which retains a terminal or a flow says so as
      `Stdio^{…}`.

      Code which cannot be changed — a Java library printing to `System.out` — is redirected
      with `.divert`, which is a diversion of the calling *thread* and the tasks spawned
      under it, not of the JVM, so a host may divert around several guests at once without
      their output crossing; `.capture` is the same with the output collected as text.
  refer turbulence.Out
  refer turbulence.Err
  refer turbulence.In
  refer turbulence.stdios
  entry .Provider   something which carries a canonical `Stdio`: a CLI invocation
    detail
        A trait with one member, `#stdio`. The `Stdio.provided` given makes a pure `Stdio`
        summonable wherever a `Provider^` is in scope, which is how `Out.println` inside a
        CLI's `execute` block finds the invocation's streams without naming them.
    entry #stdio   the provider's standard streams
  entry .provided   the `Stdio` of whatever `Stdio.Provider` is in scope
      A conditional given in the companion of the searched type, so it needs no import: a
      `Provider^` (a tracked capability such as an invocation) in scope yields its `stdio`,
      a pure value.
  entry .apply   builds a `Stdio` from JDK streams and a `Termcap`, muting any that is `null`
      `Stdio(out, err, in, termcap)`: a `null` output goes to `.MutePrintStream` and a
      `null` input to `.MuteInputStream`, which reports end of input.
  entry .MuteOutputStream   an `OutputStream` which discards every write
  entry .MutePrintStream   a `PrintStream` over `.MuteOutputStream`: what a `null` `out` or `err` becomes
  entry .MuteInputStream   an `InputStream` at end of input: `read()` is `-1` and nothing is available
  entry .Capture   what a block printed through the JVM's streams while they were captured, beside its result
    detail
        `Stdio.capture` returns `Capture(result, out, err)`: the block's value, and what
        reached `System.out` and `System.err` meanwhile, decoded as UTF-8, the encoding the
        capturing streams wrote.
    entry #result   the block's value
    entry #out   what was written to standard output, as text
    entry #err   what was written to standard error, as text
  entry .diverted   the `Stdio` this thread's `System` writes are diverted to, or `Unset`
      What a task carries to the thread it runs on. `Unset` on a thread with no diversion
      in force.
  entry .divert   runs a block with this thread's `System.out` and `System.err` redirected to a `Stdio`
      `Stdio.divert(stdio)(block)` is a diversion of the calling thread, not of the JVM. The
      first call installs one dispatcher on each of `System.out` and `System.err`, kept for
      the life of the JVM (so `System.out` is a different object after it), through which
      every write reaches the stream the writing thread is diverted to, or the original
      where it is not. A thread started under a diversion inherits it, and a parasite task
      runs under the diversion of the thread that spawned it, so a diversion follows the
      structure of the concurrency, not the accident of which thread a task lands on. On the
      way out the guest's streams are flushed and the previous diversion restored, in a
      `finally`. Scala's own `println` is thread-local already; this covers Java prints and
      anything else which writes to the `System` streams. Nothing reaches a diversion
      through `stdios.fileDescriptorStdio`, which names the process's descriptors directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap

      val buffer = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
      val guest = Stdio(java.io.PrintStream(buffer, true, "UTF-8"), null, null, basicTermcap)

      Stdio.divert(guest):
        java.lang.System.out.nn.print("routed")

      buffer.toString("UTF-8").nn.tt   // t"routed"
      ```
  entry .capture   runs a block with its `System.out` and `System.err` output collected as text
      `Stdio.capture(block)` is `.divert` into a pair of in-memory UTF-8 streams, returning a
      `Capture` of the block's result and the two texts. Output through `Out` under
      `stdios.javaLangSystemStdio` is captured too, since that `Stdio` wraps the JVM's
      streams; two concurrent captures on different tasks keep their output apart.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Stdio.capture:
        java.lang.System.out.nn.print("shown")
        7
      // Stdio.Capture(7, t"shown", t"")
      ```
  entry #termcap   what the terminal at the other end can render
  entry #out   standard output, as a `PrintStream`
  entry #err   standard error, as a `PrintStream`
  entry #in   standard input, as an `InputStream`
  entry #ready   whether a character can be read from `in` without blocking
      Consults the shared character reader over `in`, so characters already buffered inside
      its decoder count, which an `available()` check on the raw stream would miss.
  entry #readChar   reads one character from the shared reader, or `-1` at end of input
      The companion to `#ready`, for character-at-a-time consumers such as a terminal. The
      reader is shared with the `read(array: Array[Char])` overload.
  entry #write   writes bytes to standard output
  entry #print   writes text to standard output, as `out.print`
  entry #writeErr   writes bytes to standard error
  entry #printErr   writes text to standard error
  entry #read   fills a byte array from `in`, or a char array from the shared reader, returning the count
  entry #platform   whether these are the JVM's own `System.out`, `System.in` and `System.err`

entry .Out   standard output, through the `Stdio` in scope
  detail
      `Out.print` and `Out.println` render any `Printable` value — `Text`, a styled
      `Teletype`, anything with a `Printable` instance — under the `Stdio`'s `Termcap`, so
      colour and styling are emitted only where the terminal can show them, and write it to
      the `Stdio`'s `out`. The argument is a `Termcap ?=> textual`, so an expression which
      needs the termcap to render may be passed directly. `println` takes any number of
      lines and writes them under a lock, so lines from concurrent writers do not interleave.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap

      Out.println(t"Hello world")
      Out.print(t"no newline")
      Out.println()
      Out.write(t"raw\n".read[Data])
      ```

      In a daemon or a CLI the `Stdio` is the client's, so this is the only output which
      reaches the user; Scala's `println` writes to the daemon process's own `System.out`.
  refer turbulence.Stdio
  refer turbulence.Err
  entry .write   writes bytes to standard output
  entry .print   renders a `Printable` value under the terminal's `Termcap` and writes it, with no newline
  entry .println   writes each line, rendered, with a newline after each; with no argument, a newline alone
      The multi-line form needs a plain `Stdio` (not a capturing one) and holds a mutex for
      the whole call.

entry .Err   standard error, through the `Stdio` in scope
  detail
      The twin of `Out` over the `Stdio`'s `err` stream: `Err.print` and `Err.println`
      render a `Printable` value under the terminal's `Termcap` and write it, and
      `Err.write` writes bytes. `println` with several lines writes them under a lock.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap

      Err.println(t"a warning")
      ```
  refer turbulence.Stdio
  refer turbulence.Out
  entry .write   writes bytes to standard error
  entry .print   renders a `Printable` value under the terminal's `Termcap` and writes it to standard error
  entry .println   writes each line to standard error with a newline after each; with no argument, a newline alone

entry .In   standard input, through the `Stdio` in scope
  detail
      `In.read(array)` fills a byte array from the `Stdio`'s `in` and returns the count read
      (or `-1` at end of input), and `In.close()` closes it. Character-level reading goes
      through `Stdio#readChar` and `Stdio#ready`; a whole line through a reader over
      `summon[Stdio].in`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap

      val buffer = new scala.Array[Byte](1024)
      val count = In.read(buffer)
      ```
  refer turbulence.Stdio
  entry .read   fills a byte array from standard input, returning the count or `-1` at the end
  entry .close   closes standard input

entry .stdios   the standard-stream configurations, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which streams `Out`, `Err` and `In` use is decided by the `Stdio` given in scope, and
      the choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is
      imported by name. All but `muteStdio` need a `Termcap` in scope too, chosen from
      `termcapDefinitions`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap
      ```

      A CLI or daemon invocation provides its own `Stdio` through `Stdio.Provider`, so an
      `execute` block imports none of these.
  refer turbulence.Stdio
  entry .muteStdio   a `Stdio` which discards all output and is at end of input, with the basic `Termcap`
      For a test, or a program which must not print: `out` and `err` are `Stdio.MutePrintStream`
      and `in` is `Stdio.MuteInputStream`. Needs no `Termcap`.
  entry .javaLangSystemStdio   the JVM's `System.out`, `System.err` and `System.in`, with the `Termcap` in scope
      Output written through it is subject to `Stdio.divert` and `Stdio.capture`, since
      those redirect the JVM's streams.
  entry .fileDescriptorStdio   the process's own file descriptors 0, 1 and 2, with the `Termcap` in scope
      Fresh `PrintStream`s and a `FileInputStream` over `FileDescriptor.out`, `err` and `in`:
      the streams as the operating system handed them to the process, bypassing anything a
      JVM or a host has installed in `System.out`. Nothing written through it reaches a
      `Stdio.divert`.
  entry .wasiStdio   a `Stdio` over the WASI CLI stream resources, for a Wasm component
      In `turbulence.wasi`, with the `Termcap` in scope. Each write obtains the `stdout` or
      `stderr` stream handle, calls its `blocking-write-and-flush`, and disposes of the
      handle; a read obtains `stdin` and calls `blocking-read`, blocking until a byte is
      available, with a closed stream read as end of input. `print` and `printErr` send
      the text's UTF-8 bytes directly rather than through the `PrintStream`s. The given is
      `inline`, so the WASI imports materialise at the site which summons it, which must
      also see `wasiApis.wasiCliApi`; summon it once, in the component's entry point.

entry .Confluence   merges several pull endpoints into one, in arrival order
  detail
      `Confluence(sources*)` is fan-in. A pull architecture has no `select` over blocked
      refills, so the merge is the honest cost of multiplexing: one pump task per input,
      each pulling transfer-sized credits into a shared bounded queue — a full queue parks
      the pumps, so slow consumption backpressures every input uniformly — and a reader
      endpoint which parks while the queue is empty. The merged stream ends when every input
      has ended; an input's failure is rethrown from the reader's next refill. In
      `turbulence.async`: it needs the medium's `Addressable`, a `Buffering`, and a parasite
      `Monitor` and `Probate`, so it runs inside `supervise`. The pumps are children of the
      enclosing scope, and cancelling it releases pumps parked on a merge nobody is reading.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate

      supervise:
        Confluence(Stream(t"one"), Stream(t"two"), Stream(t"three")).memoize
      ```

      A region-stable source (a fixed in-memory buffer) is shared by reference; a transient
      one is snapshotted block by block before its window is reused, in blocks of
      `buffering.transfer(substrate)`.
  refer turbulence.Divergence
  refer turbulence.convey
  refer turbulence.Relay
  entry .apply   the merged stream of any number of `(Stream[medium] over Credit)^` sources

entry .Divergence   delivers one pull endpoint to several subscribers, each chunk shared
  detail
      `Divergence(source, count)` is fan-out: one pump task pulls the source and delivers
      every chunk to each of `count` subscriber endpoints through a bounded ring per
      subscriber. Chunks are materialised once and shared read-only between subscribers; a
      full ring parks the pump, so the slowest subscriber gates the source, which is the
      right backpressure for replication. A subscriber which closes its stream closes its
      ring, releasing the pump and discarding later deliveries, so an abandoned slow
      subscriber cannot wedge the rest. A source failure is rethrown from each subscriber's
      next refill. In `turbulence.async`: needs the medium's `Addressable`, a `Buffering`,
      and a parasite `Monitor` and `Probate`, so it runs inside `supervise`; the source is
      consumed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate

      supervise:
        val subscribers = Divergence(Stream(t"shared"), 2)
        subscribers(0).memoize
      ```

      The result is an `IndexedSeq` of `count` streams, each an exclusive endpoint drained
      by one consumer; read them on separate tasks or one after another.
  refer turbulence.Confluence
  entry .apply   `count` subscriber streams over one consumed source

entry .convey   pumps a stream into an intake on its own task
  detail
      An extension in `turbulence.async` on a consumed `(Stream[medium] over transport)^`:
      `stream.convey(intake)` is `stream.pump(intake)` run on a parasite task, returning the
      `Task[Unit]`, for fire-and-forget transfers and for pipelines with an asynchronous
      boundary, where this is the one pumping thread. Both endpoints move onto the task; the
      caller keeps neither. Needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate`, so it runs inside `supervise`,
      and cancelling the scope releases a pump blocked on an empty conduit.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import threads.platformThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      supervise:
        val output = java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()
        val sink = summon[(java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream is Sink by Data over Credit)^]
        t"hello".read[Data].source[Data].convey(sink.intake(output)).await()
      ```
  refer turbulence.Sink
  refer turbulence.Confluence

entry .WasiCliApi   the WIT interface of the WASI CLI world, as a type: `Interface in Wit at "/turbulence/cli.wit"`
  detail
      In `turbulence.wasi`. The type the `stdios.wasiStdio` navigation is typechecked
      against, and which the `call` materialiser consults for the `get-stdout`, `get-stderr`
      and `get-stdin` imports and the stream resources' methods. Its value is
      `wasiApis.wasiCliApi`.
  refer turbulence.wasiApis

entry .wasiApis   the WASI interface definitions turbulence links against, each a given to import by name
  detail
      In `turbulence.wasi`. A Wasm component which summons `stdios.wasiStdio` must have
      `wasiCliApi` visible at that site, since the stream calls expand there.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import wasiApis.wasiCliApi
      import stdios.wasiStdio
      import termcapDefinitions.basicTermcap
      ```
  entry .wasiCliApi   the `WasiCliApi` interface, loaded from the `/turbulence/cli.wit` classpath resource
      `Interface[Wit](cp"/turbulence/cli.wit")`: the resource is checked at compile time, and
      the WIT it holds defines the `stdout`, `stderr` and `stdin` imports with their
      `output-stream` and `input-stream` resources.
